question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94189/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "331cb610-6168-5161-b821-237c713dedaa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94189"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140941/selection/cb99d30519dd09b4881cfa847e1667e30fbdf9087cd5f0eb2fbe8b7c7982bbc0" :answer/id #uuid "0ffcc2ed-2f10-5d03-80f0-a1aadb2c9b69" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140941/selection/d10854890c93038351d40f9c891e95b81e060ee50a08baca4e6e502054b47a84" :answer/id #uuid "1c85d9b2-0841-5a20-9f5a-727f5ca87ff9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140941/selection/8a34816a7be14e79afe6471d79d6fa76c027268dc63bcb9117aa99c4cb679e4e" :answer/id #uuid "41227c04-8102-5310-91b1-86a41d585c2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140941/selection/800b03794bd969c46e56786e3e68ba9d41d436ae239ada6e43830df216a3cded" :answer/id #uuid "1aa93528-9c85-5b3d-a104-35ef4909161f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140941/selection/3411476fbe967fe8183dd1241431f24be40d3a5bda06eadf2424571955cbd209" :answer/id #uuid "32e5b43d-4bb3-5773-8876-e02a6e70068c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140941/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "79ead08f-a666-5db0-a4ab-f8f4f7b9d7fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140941/selection/cb99d30519dd09b4881cfa847e1667e30fbdf9087cd5f0eb2fbe8b7c7982bbc0" :answer-field/choices ["q-140941/selection/cb99d30519dd09b4881cfa847e1667e30fbdf9087cd5f0eb2fbe8b7c7982bbc0" "q-140941/selection/d10854890c93038351d40f9c891e95b81e060ee50a08baca4e6e502054b47a84" "q-140941/selection/8a34816a7be14e79afe6471d79d6fa76c027268dc63bcb9117aa99c4cb679e4e" "q-140941/selection/800b03794bd969c46e56786e3e68ba9d41d436ae239ada6e43830df216a3cded" "q-140941/selection/3411476fbe967fe8183dd1241431f24be40d3a5bda06eadf2424571955cbd209"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140941" :question/id #uuid "7f3efd2f-662c-53a4-b995-cf7a32bbae74" :question/math-academy-id "q-140941" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140941/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "331cb610-6168-5161-b821-237c713dedaa"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140941"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-93738"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94190"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $P$ above. Find the matrix of the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,Ax$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $x=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2}]^{T}$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $y=[{y}_{1},{y}_{2}]^{T}$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\nx=Py.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8177"] :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066712/assets/57718ef2e6ecd26138c4c6a1b817d1129ad95a7473bf2e7d1611fdc64391e5c8.png) express the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})= x_1^2-x_2^2\\,$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066712/assets/12e6df2cc03a95228ac8d54512772c914f3283f1f62d814f2e996de67c93a041.png)\n\ngives the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})^{2}-(2{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-4{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8178"] :question/problem "Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066712/assets/309c32ea89b3e3216e79962d305ef2611eeb691342ae07f7659aff52c218c5b3.png) express the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})= x_1x_2+2x_2^2$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066712/assets/7ea02af91b7b066c7aafa293882f6e2f9f81256fe8b0a30f33f2f4403142d928.png) \n\ngives the following expressions for $y_1$ and $y_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy solving the second equation for $x_1$ and substituting it into the first equation, we obtain the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into our quadratic form expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(-{y}_{2})(-{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})+2(-{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{2}{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{1}^{2}+8{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+8{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =2{y}_{1}^{2}+9{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+10{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8179"] :question/problem "Consider the matrices $A$ and $P$ below. Find the matrix of the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$ in the basis that consists of the columns of $P.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066712/assets/8c8f1626eafc81b4700a0ddc015c210508d4db464970abc706831ea86659e8da.png)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $\\mathbf{x}=[x_1, x_2]^T$ is a vector given by its coordinates in the standard basis and $\\mathbf{y}=[ y_1, y_2 ]^T$ is the same vector but given in the coordinates relative to the basis that consists of the columns of $P,$ then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = P\\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & =({y}^{T}{P}^{T})\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}\\,AP)\\,y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that the matrix of our form in the new basis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\,AP & ={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
